Chooses temporary files' names at random, from getentropy, as glibc and
musl do: mkstemp, mkostemps, mkdtemp and their kin tried 000000,
000001 and on in turn, so the next name could be guessed and taken
first, and finding a free one took a call per name already used.
tmpnam used rand(). mktemp, which ended the program, now works too.
On failure the X's are put back, as glibc does.

diff -ruN a/options/ansi/generic/stdio.cpp b/options/ansi/generic/stdio.cpp
--- a/options/ansi/generic/stdio.cpp
+++ b/options/ansi/generic/stdio.cpp
@@ -832,8 +832,11 @@
 	static thread_local char internalBuffer[L_tmpnam];
 	char *result = buf ? buf : internalBuffer;
 
-	for (size_t i = 0; i < 100; i++) {
-		int ret = snprintf(result, L_tmpnam, "/tmp/tmpnam_%06X", rand() & 0xFFFFFF);
+	for (int i = 0; i < mlibc::TEMP_TRIES; i++) {
+		char x[6];
+		if (mlibc::random_name(x))
+			return nullptr;
+		int ret = snprintf(result, L_tmpnam, "/tmp/tmpnam_%.6s", x);
 		if (ret < 18)
 			return nullptr;
 
